7047-5.2.1 General
7048-
7049-The <text:page-sequence> element contains at least one <text:page> element. If there is more than one <text:page> element, the text:master-page-name attribute of the first <text:page> element specifies the default master style for a document. 
7050-
7051-The <text:page-sequence> element is usable within the following element: <office:text> 3.4.
--
7369-Consumers shall collapse white space characters that occur in 
7370-
7371-•a <text:p> or <text:h> element (so-called paragraph elements), and  
7372-
7373:•in their descendant elements, if the OpenDocument schema permits <text:s> 6.1.3, <text:tab> 6.1.4 or <text:line-break> 6.1.4 as element content.. 
7374-
7375-Collapsing white space characters is defined by the following algorithm:
7376-
7377-1)Descendant <text:ruby> elements are replaced with their <text:ruby-base> child elements. 
7378-
7379:2)Descendant elements of the paragraph element which are not <text:s>, <text:tab> or <text:line-break> elements and for which the OpenDocument schema does not permit <text:s>, <text:tab> and <text:line-break> as child elements are removed from the paragraph element. 
7380-
7381:3)Descendant elements of the paragraph element for which the OpenDocument schema permits <text:s>, <text:tab> and <text:line-break> as child elements are replaced by their character data and <text:s>, <text:tab> and <text:line-break> element children. 
7382-
7383-4)The following [UNICODE] characters are replaced by a “ “ (U+0020, SPACE) character: 
7384-HORIZONTAL TABULATION (U+0009) 
7385-
7386-CARRIAGE RETURN (U+000D) 
7387-
7388-LINE FEED (U+000A) 
7389-
7390-5)Leading “ “ (U+0020, SPACE) characters at the start of the resulting text and trailing SPACE characters at the end of the resulting text are removed. 
7391-
7392-6)Sequences of “ “ (U+0020, SPACE) characters are replaced by a single “ “ (U+0020, SPACE) character.  
7393-
7394:7)The remaining <text:s>, <text:tab> and <text:line-break> elements are interpreted as the [UNICODE] white space characters they represent. 
7395-
7396-OpenDocument producers shall produce paragraph elements that, when consumed according to this algorithm, result in the expected amount of white space.
7397-
7398-OpenDocument consumers shall either process white space such that the result is equivalent to the result of the given algorithm, or implement a variation that increases interoperability with popular OpenDocument 1.2 producers. The variation replaces step 2 of the algorithm with steps 2a and 2b:
7399-
7400-2a) Descendant elements of the paragraph element that are mark elements (<text:change> 5.5.8.4, <text:change-end> 5.5.8.3, <text:change-start> 5.5.8.2, <text:bookmark> 6.2.1.2, <text:bookmark-end> 6.2.1.4, <text:bookmark-start> 6.2.1.3, <text:reference-mark> 6.2.2.2, <text:reference-mark-end> 6.2.2.4, <text:reference-mark-start> 6.2.2.3, <text:toc-mark> 8.1.4, <text:toc-mark-end> 8.1.3, <text:toc-mark-start> 8.1.2, <text:user-index-mark> 8.1.7, <text:user-index-mark-end> 8.1.6, <text:user-index-mark-start> 8.1.5, <text:alphabetical-index-mark> 8.1.10, <text:alphabetical-index-mark-end> 8.1.9, <text:alphabetical-index-mark-start> 8.1.8) are removed from the paragraph element.
7401-
7402:2b) Descendant elements of the paragraph element which are not <text:s>, <text:tab> or <text:line-break> elements and for which the OpenDocument schema does not permit <text:s>, <text:tab> and <text:line-break> as child elements are replaced with a hypothetical <text:s text:c="0"/> element. 
7403-
7404-Note: XML formatting software that does not implement the ODF white space rules may introduce or remove spaces.
